**Decision Making** — Quick Facts for UCAT ANZ candidates sitting in New Zealand

**What is UCAT ANZ Decision Making?**
Decision Making (DM) is the second cognitive subtest in the standard UCAT ANZ sequence. It evaluates your ability to apply logic to evaluate arguments, weigh evidence, reach a conclusion, and judge the merits of competing options. DM is the LARGEST UCAT ANZ subtest by allocated testing time (29% of the total). The subtest runs for a target testing time of 31 minutes with 29 items (the question count varies year to year and is published by the UCAT ANZ Consortium before each cycle), so demand a per-item budget of ~64 seconds — more than double the Verbal Reasoning per-item time. The on-screen calculator IS provided in DM (unlike VR).

**At a glance:**
- 29 items, 31 minutes (≈64 sec/item)
- On-screen calculator available (use sparingly; mental math is faster)
- Five question archetypes: syllogisms, logical puzzles, probability, Venn diagrams, conditional reasoning (assumptions + arguments)
- One item at a time; flag and move on if stuck past the per-item budget
- Marks: +1 correct, 0 incorrect (no negative marking)

**Why DM matters for offers:**
Decision Making is the largest subtest by allocated time and contributes the largest share to your overall UCAT ANZ score (1,200-3,600 scale). New Zealand universities (Auckland, Otago) use the overall score as one component of a multi-stage admissions process. A strong DM score (≥650) is the strongest single signal of analytical ability. The DM score sits alongside Verbal Reasoning (see topic ucat-nz-001), Quantitative Reasoning (see topic ucat-nz-003) and Situational Judgement (see topic ucat-nz-004).

⚡ **Quick rule:** Practise the 5 archetypes in isolation first, then mixed sets. The official UCAT ANZ practice tests on ucat.edu.au are the only authentic source.

